This bug appears when trying use an interval selection over an ordinal axis that has a color encoding. Seems to be broken when the interaction happens over the marks themselves, but works fine when over empty canvas. 

```json
{
  "config": {"view": {"continuousWidth": 400, "continuousHeight": 300}},
  "data": {"url": "data/seattle-weather.csv"},
  "mark": {"type": "bar", "cornerRadiusTopLeft": 3, "cornerRadiusTopRight": 3},
  "encoding": {
    "color": {
      "condition": {
        "field": "weather",
        "type": "nominal",
        "param": "drag-brush"
      },
      "value": "lightgray"
    },
    "x": {"field": "date", "timeUnit": "month", "type": "ordinal"},
    "y": {"aggregate": "count", "type": "quantitative"}
  },
  "params": [
    {"name": "drag-brush", "select": {"type": "interval", "encodings": ["x"]}}
  ] 
}
```
[Vega Editor](https://vega.github.io/editor/#/url/vega-)

![2023-01-06 13 42 39](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/30129761/211078180-f9e3cffb-5998-4607-b71e-7e090fa71521.gif)

consider (in a vl.json file);

```"layer": [
                {   "comment": "text (data) at (last_x, y), next to y2-axis, i.e. entry point into chart",
                    "mark": {
                        "type": "text",
                        "align": "left",
                        "dx": 5,
                        "dy": 0
                    },
                    "encoding": {
                        "comment": "gives weird runtime error",
                        "text": {
                            "type": "quantitative",
                            "field": "data"
                        },
                       ...
                    }
                }
            ]
```
Since *.json files do not have a comment syntax, it would be nice to allow a dummy field, e.g. 'comment' everywhere in a *.vl.json file.

It works already (maybe accidentally) in some places, but not everywhere. Here in the encoding section it produces a weird runtime error message by obviously trying to process the comment text:
 TypeError: Cannot use 'in' operator to search for 'scale' in gives weird runtime error ..."

